WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:12.920
A GOOGLE CRISIS:
The 

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:14.290
date is July of 2001.

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:21.460
Out of the blue, Larry Page, co-founder and
CEO of Google, sends a cryptic email summoning

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:28.500
all Google engineers and project managers
to meet outside his office to share some unexpected

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:29.500
news.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:33.579
Within a matter of minutes, all 130 of Google’s
engineers and project managers would form

00:00:33.579 --> 00:00:38.140
a huddle around Page’s office anxious what
the news could be.

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:40.160
Did Google get sued by Yahoo?

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.940
Was AOL threatening a hostile takeover?

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:45.450
Was the company about to run out of funding?

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:50.760
As these thoughts raced through engineers
minds, Page candidly stepped out of his office.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:56.800
And with his usual horse voice, he would firmly
announce that all project managers were fired

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:58.809
effective immediately.

00:00:58.809 --> 00:01:04.189
All engineers were to report to some newly
hired engineer named Wayne Rosing.

00:01:04.189 --> 00:01:06.090
Everyone was stunned.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:10.009
This wasn’t about the state of Google or
the future of the company.

00:01:10.009 --> 00:01:13.140
This was about Page’s personal hatred for
managers.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:18.700
You see, Page strongly felt that Google had
hired the best engineers in the world and

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:23.079
that these brilliant individuals didn’t
need untechnical morons telling them what

00:01:23.079 --> 00:01:24.079
to do.

00:01:24.079 --> 00:01:28.920
He felt that engineers should be able to choose
what they work and set their own paces, and

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:31.520
he was hoping that Google’s engineers would
agree.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:36.909
But, to his surprise, it was actually the
engineers that would speak back.

00:01:36.909 --> 00:01:41.610
One engineer named Ron Dolin snapped back
that what Page was doing was “completely

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:44.380
ridiculous” and “totally unprofessional”.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:46.270
Page as gobsmacked.

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:51.240
Surely, other engineers would understand the
freedom of having no managers and support

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:52.380
the purge.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:57.990
Maybe, they just needed a bit of time to adjust,
so Page woud adjourn the meeting and hoped

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:01.220
things would settle down, but they didn’t.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:06.060
Within just a few weeks, projects would start
lacking resources, redundancy would fall off

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:10.830
a cliff, and engineers were craving 1 on 1
feedback from a direct superior.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:15.890
Unsurprisingly, VC funders were pissed, and
it didn’t take long for HR to step in.

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:21.350
They would reinstate all of Google’s fired
project managers and before you knew it, there

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:25.379
was someone new on the chopping block: co
founder Larry Page.

00:02:25.379 --> 00:02:31.920
A man named Eric Schmidt was to become the
new CEO and Chairman of Google and Larry Page

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.420
would be relieved of all duties.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:38.950
Instead, he was to take on a more advisory
role behind Eric.

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:44.480
Page was devastated, he had just been Steve
Jobsed, kicked out of his own company and

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:47.739
basically given a pity role as an advisor.

00:02:47.739 --> 00:02:52.920
But, looking back, this was for the best as
Eric Schmidt would take Google from being

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:56.690
a brilliant startup to being a tech empire.

00:02:56.690 --> 00:03:01.110
He would lead the company for the next 10
years and make Google the leader in email,

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:06.060
video sharing, search, maps, cloud, browsers,
mobile, and more.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:11.230
He would grow a fortune of $25 billion for
himself as he spearheaded Google into becoming

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:13.860
a multi hundred billion dollar company.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:19.049
But, almost as magically as he appeared at
Google, he would vanish as this is the story

00:03:19.049 --> 00:03:26.470
of the forgotten CEO that saved Google from
its own founder: Eric Schmidt.

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:32.830
THE WHITE KNIGHT:
While Eric was an outsider, he was by no means

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:34.170
unqualified.

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:40.489
Eric was born in Falls Church, Virginia on
April 27, 1955 to two academics.

00:03:40.489 --> 00:03:45.959
His mother Eleanor had a masters in psychology
way back in the 1950s and his father was an

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:48.879
extremely well respected economics professor.

00:03:48.879 --> 00:03:53.220
He not only taught at Virginia Tech and John
Hopkins University, but he would go on to

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:56.659
serve in the US Treasury under President Richard
Nixon.

00:03:56.659 --> 00:04:01.459
Little to say, Eric had a pretty strong background
which allowed him to excel at school and attend

00:04:01.459 --> 00:04:02.659
Princeton University.

00:04:02.659 --> 00:04:07.879
There he would receive a bachelors in electrical
engineering in 1976 before moving onto UC

00:04:07.879 --> 00:04:09.519
Berkeley to get a masters.

00:04:09.519 --> 00:04:15.030
Eric got a masters in Electrical Engineering
and Computer Science in 1979, but it seems

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:20.019
that one of his graduate school projects was
even more valuable than the education itself.

00:04:20.019 --> 00:04:25.340
You see, Eric would design and implement a
network called Berknet that linked up the

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:30.760
computer centers of two departments, the CS
department and the EECS department.

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:35.840
This project would actually evolve into becoming
a PhD thesis about managing distributed software

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:36.840
development.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:42.130
And Eric would cash in on this PhD in 1982
also from the UC Berkeley.

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:47.050
During his academic years, he would jump around
a bunch of IT companies from Bell Labs to

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:49.440
Xerox’s famous Palo Alto Research Center.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:54.870
But, his real career in tech didn’t start
till 1983 when he joined Sun Microsystems

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:57.380
as their first software manager.

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:02.280
This is quite ironic as this was the exact
position that Larry Page would try to eliminate

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:07.600
at Google 20 years later, but let’s just
say that Eric would more than prove that this

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:11.060
position was more than just a placeholder
at Sun.

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:15.600
Eric would rise up the ranks like a madman
first being promoted to director of software

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:19.870
engineering before being promoted to vice
president of software products and then vice

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:21.001
president of general systems.

00:05:21.001 --> 00:05:22.001
Eric followed this up with a promotion to
president and finally a promotion to Chief

00:05:22.001 --> 00:05:23.001
Technological Officer.

00:05:23.001 --> 00:05:26.560
During his 14 years at Sun, Eric oversaw the
creation of one of the most respected tech

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:28.380
empires of the 1990s.

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:34.590
Now of course, Sun would get crushed by the
dotcom crash and be completely forgotten about,

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:36.410
but that’s a wholenother story.

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:41.070
I guess you could say that it’s a good thing
that Eric left at the top in 1997 and took

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:44.289
on a position as CEO and Chairman of Novell.

00:05:44.289 --> 00:05:48.930
If you’re not familiar with Novell, they
were actually the second largest producer

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:52.160
of software at one point, only beaten out
by Microsoft.

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:58.000
But, by the late 90s, they were a declining
giant, and Eric was brought in to turn around

00:05:58.000 --> 00:05:59.000
the company.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:00.000
He would oversee a major transition in network
technology from IPX to TCP while trying to

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.000
fend of Microsoft.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:04.020
It seems that he was actually pretty successful
as the company was in a good enough position

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:08.110
to acquire some beaten down dotcom companies
like Cambridge Technology Partners.

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:13.560
But, Eric wasn’t able to get much further
than that as right after this acquisition,

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:17.570
he would be called into babysit Larry Page
and Sergey Brin.

00:06:17.570 --> 00:06:23.740
A SAVIOR STEPS IN:
Like Steve Jobs, Larry Page and Sergey Brin

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:29.060
were actually able to choose who would take
on the position of CEO, and it was them who

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:30.370
chose Eric.

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:33.610
Thankfully for Google, Eric wasn’t another
John Sculley.

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:39.160
While Google’s board and investors did pressure
Eric to reign in Larry and Sergey, Eric didn’t

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:40.530
give into this pressure.

00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:45.220
You see, though Eric was hired to be a babysitter,
he felt that it was much more suitable for

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:48.280
him to take on the role of personal assistant.

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:52.380
While Eric was exceptionally qualified, probably
even more qualified than Larry and Sergey

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:56.840
at the time, he recognized that it was not
his place to inject his own vision for the

00:06:56.840 --> 00:06:57.840
company.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:02.370
At the end of the day, this was Larry and
Sergey’s company and he was simply hired

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:07.840
to help them execute their vision without
pissing off the board or investors, and that’s

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:09.400
exactly what he did.

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:13.210
After he took over, he would give Larry and
Sergey full freedom to continue leading the

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:16.590
company from a technical and visionary standpoint.

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:20.750
Eric would simply take care of all of the
daily nuances and obstacles that came along

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:23.580
with running and scaling a startup.

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:28.180
Things like addressing investors, addressing
the board, handling public relations, dealing

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:34.650
with HR, scaling the worforce, resolving conflicts,
and launching products on time and on budget.

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:38.389
Basically, all of the boring stuff that comes
along with being CEO.

00:07:38.389 --> 00:07:43.870
But, while boring, this is the lifeblood of
any company and especially an up and coming

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:45.620
startup like Google.

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:50.470
Also, if you have any doubts about just how
much freedom Eric gave Larry and Sergey, here’s

00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:53.110
how many acquisitions went down at Google.

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:57.330
Larry and Sergey would find something that
interested them, they would buy the company,

00:07:57.330 --> 00:08:01.139
and Eric would find out after everything was
said and done.

00:08:01.139 --> 00:08:05.190
This is actually how Google ended up with
Android and Google Maps.

00:08:05.190 --> 00:08:12.630
A regular CEO would probably be quite bothered
by this, but Eric figured that Larry and Sergey

00:08:12.630 --> 00:08:16.139
were the most incentivized to not ruin the
company.

00:08:16.139 --> 00:08:20.870
Aside from giving Larry and Sergey space,
Eric also formed key relationships with other

00:08:20.870 --> 00:08:21.970
tech companies.

00:08:21.970 --> 00:08:26.870
For example, in late 2006, he would join Apple’s
board of directors right before the launch

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:27.880
of the iPhone.

00:08:27.880 --> 00:08:33.560
But, despite all this freedom, for the first
few years, Larry always had a level of distrust

00:08:33.560 --> 00:08:34.560
with Eric.

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:38.930
I mean after all, Eric was literally hired
to replace Larry.

00:08:38.930 --> 00:08:44.260
But, overtime, Larry would grow comfortable
with delegating basically everything to Eric

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:45.260
other than the vision.

00:08:45.260 --> 00:08:46.260
A great example of this was Larry’s waning
meeting attendance.

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:50.140
Larry was never a fan of meetings, but he
was intent on attending them in the early

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:54.160
days as he wanted to make sure that Eric didn’t
overrule his voice.

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:59.459
But, by 2007, he would stop attending meetings
altogether as he trusted that Eric would carry

00:08:59.459 --> 00:09:01.089
out what he wanted.

00:09:01.089 --> 00:09:06.740
By 2008, Larry would also stop doing interviews
altogether stating that that was what Eric

00:09:06.740 --> 00:09:07.740
was for.

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:12.760
Larry and Eric were getting along better than
the board could have ever imagined, but this

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:17.180
golden period didn’t last forever at Google.

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:20.940
THE FALLOUT:
As Larry and Sergey got to know Eric better,

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:27.110
they realized that while he was exceptional
at execution, he wasn’t a risk taker.

00:09:27.110 --> 00:09:31.940
Sure, he was willing to let Larry and Sergey
mess around with acquisitions and run crazy

00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:35.149
April Fools day pranks with Gmail and YouTube.

00:09:35.149 --> 00:09:39.820
But when it came to something that could truly
threaten the company, Eric would always choose

00:09:39.820 --> 00:09:44.410
the conservative way out and one of the best
examples of this was with Chrome.

00:09:44.410 --> 00:09:48.870
Chrome was pretty late to the browser scene
having not been released till late 2008.

00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:51.870
But, this wasn’t because Chrome was an afterthought.

00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:54.990
It was because Eric wouldn’t allow for Chrome.

00:09:54.990 --> 00:10:00.490
Larry and Sergey had actually pitched Chrome
to Eric for 6 full years, but he always rejected

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:02.230
the idea stating that the space was simply
too monopolized and saturated.

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:05.959
It wasn’t until Larry and Sergey went behind
Eric’s back and secretly developed a Chrome

00:10:05.959 --> 00:10:09.500
prototype that Eric simply gave up on trying
to stop them.

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:15.080
Now of course, Chrome would go on to be a
roaring success, so this was definitely something

00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:19.459
that Eric got wrong, but it’s not what pushed
him out.

00:10:19.459 --> 00:10:22.290
What pushed him out was his stance on China.

00:10:22.290 --> 00:10:27.530
As you know, the CCP is the CCP, and you either
play by their rules or you get out.

00:10:27.530 --> 00:10:32.270
Throughout the early 2000s, Eric had worked
painstakingly hard to play by the CCP’s

00:10:32.270 --> 00:10:35.630
rules and get Google launched in China in
2006.

00:10:35.630 --> 00:10:41.340
But, by the late 2000s, China was changing
up the rules and they wanted Google to censor

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:42.470
certain topics.

00:10:42.470 --> 00:10:46.710
While not ideal, Eric was more than happy
to do this.

00:10:46.710 --> 00:10:51.160
After all, he felt that it was more important
for Google to stay relevant in what he describes

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:54.370
as the world’s largest consumer marketplace.

00:10:54.370 --> 00:10:56.840
Larry and Sergey, however, disagreed.

00:10:56.840 --> 00:11:01.649
They felt that it was more important for Google
to stick by their motto of “Don’t be Evil”

00:11:01.649 --> 00:11:03.520
and not allow for censorship.

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:07.570
Larry and Sergey would end up getting their
way and Google would leave China in 2010,

00:11:07.570 --> 00:11:08.600
but that didn’t shake the feeling.

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:13.630
For the first time, Larry felt that Eric was
pulling Google in a different direction, a

00:11:13.630 --> 00:11:16.160
direction that he didn’t want to go down.

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:21.769
It was clear to him that it was time for Eric
to go and for himself to take the reigns once

00:11:21.769 --> 00:11:22.769
again.

00:11:22.769 --> 00:11:26.500
And given that Larry now had a relatively
positive relationship with the board, thanks

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:30.920
to Eric, he was able to lobby for himself
to become CEO once again.

00:11:30.920 --> 00:11:34.530
Eric who was quickly approaching 60 didn’t
really resist.

00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:39.410
He’d been there for nearly 10 years and
it felt like a natural time to move on.

00:11:39.410 --> 00:11:43.840
So, he would leave of with the remark, “I
believe Larry is ready, I'm sure he'll do

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:45.800
a fantastic job.”

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:52.730
And with that, Larry was CEO once again, but
the truth was that he wasn’t really ready.

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:53.730
He instantly turned back to his old tendencies,
but this time he had a different target.

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:57.459
He felt that the biggest limiter for Google
wasn’t managers, but rather shareholders.

00:11:57.459 --> 00:12:04.690
While he may very well have been right, let’s
just say it’s not a great idea to make shareholders

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:07.430
an enemy as public CEO.

00:12:07.430 --> 00:12:12.610
Larry would learn this the hard way, and he
would only last a few years as CEO.

00:12:12.610 --> 00:12:18.070
Pretty soon, he would promote Sundar Pichai
to CEO leading us to the modern day Google.

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:23.860
ERIC’S LEGACY:
At the end of the day, Larry and Sergey truly

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:29.089
are the visionaries behind Google, but their
visions would have never materialized if it

00:12:29.089 --> 00:12:30.420
wasn’t for Eric.

00:12:30.420 --> 00:12:35.149
The reality was that while Larry and Sergey
were brilliant engineers and forward thinkers,

00:12:35.149 --> 00:12:38.430
they just weren’t all that great businessmen.

00:12:38.430 --> 00:12:43.649
And if it wasn’t clear the first time around,
it was definitely clear the second time around.

00:12:43.649 --> 00:12:48.899
They needed someone to focus and execute on
their ideas and that’s exactly what Eric

00:12:48.899 --> 00:12:49.899
did.

00:12:49.899 --> 00:12:55.100
To be honest, Eric still had a few good years
in him at Google, but I suppose it all worked

00:12:55.100 --> 00:12:56.360
out fine anyway.

00:12:56.360 --> 00:13:00.769
All of the stock that Eric earned during his
tenure made him the 72nd richest person in

00:13:00.769 --> 00:13:04.170
the world with a net worth of $21 billion.

00:13:04.170 --> 00:13:08.221
But, that’s arguably the smallest part of
his legacy.

00:13:08.221 --> 00:13:09.780
Did you know about Eric Schmidt?

